'use strict';
// Original educational scenarios; interventions remain subject to local scope/protocol.
const SCENARIO_CASES=[
 {id:'saw',dispatch:'Industrial park, 14:20. “Worker cut his leg.” One patient; caller says the machine is now off.',scene:'A supervisor is holding a shop towel against the thigh. A powered saw is stopped, but its electrical supply has not been isolated. Blood is pooling under the patient.',safe:'Keep clear until machinery is isolated and the work area is confirmed safe; arrange prompt access to the patient.',unsafe:['Assume a stopped blade cannot restart and step into the machinery area.','Enter immediately because severe bleeding overrides rescuer safety.','Cancel fire assistance because there is only one patient.'],clear:'The supervisor isolates the power and fire personnel confirm safe access. You can reach the patient without entering the machinery.',ppe:['Gloves','Mask plus eye/face protection for blood splash'],resources:['ALS intercept without delaying hemorrhage control','Fire assistance for machinery isolation and safe access'],equipment:['Commercial tourniquet and hemorrhage-control supplies','Stretcher and thermal protection'],extra:['Air ambulance automatically, without assessing geography','Remove embedded debris with forceps'],contact:'A 38-year-old opens his eyes and follows a command only after you speak loudly. He does not interact spontaneously. A deep thigh wound continues to spurt through firm direct pressure.',avpu:'V',avpuWhy:'He responds to voice. Following a command after verbal stimulation does not make him spontaneously alert.',findings:['Life-threatening external thigh bleeding','He speaks clearly when prompted; no gurgling','RR 26 with adequate chest rise; SpO₂ 95%','Pulse 132, BP 84/56, cool clammy skin'],x:'Apply a commercial tourniquet for the uncontrolled life-threatening extremity bleed; verify bleeding stops.',a:'Continue to monitor the patent airway while controlling hemorrhage.',b:'Assess breathing effectiveness and oxygenation; support or titrate oxygen as indicated by protocol.',c:'Treat shock, prevent heat loss, reassess bleeding and perfusion, and expedite transport.',why:['The ongoing extremity hemorrhage is immediately lethal and is not controlled by pressure. Do not delay a tourniquet to complete history taking.','Clear speech indicates current patency, but deteriorating mental status can threaten airway protection.','A fast rate alone does not prove inadequate ventilation; assess depth, effort, and oxygenation.','Control the cause of blood loss and limit delays; a splint and full history are secondary.'],transport:'Begin prompt transport to an appropriate trauma center under regional triage guidance while continuing care; coordinate ALS en route.',trend:'The tourniquet has stopped visible bleeding, but the patient stops responding to voice and now responds only to an appropriate painful stimulus. Breathing becomes shallow.',next:'Reclassify AVPU as P, reassess airway and ventilation immediately, and assist breathing if inadequate while continuing rapid transport.',reason:'Bleeding control does not reverse blood already lost. Worsening responsiveness and shallow breathing require a fresh primary assessment.'},
 {id:'overdose',dispatch:'Apartment, 01:10. “Won’t wake up.” A friend reports possible opioid use.',scene:'Police confirm no weapons or aggression. You see uncapped needles near the patient and vomit beside the head. There is no hazardous atmosphere.',safe:'Use a safe approach, avoid sharps, and establish a clear working area before patient contact.',unsafe:['Reach blindly under the patient to clear the area.','Put on gloves and collect needles by hand before assessing breathing.','Wait for a drug-identification test before entering the secured room.'],clear:'Your partner creates a clear working area without handling exposed needles. The patient is accessible.',ppe:['Gloves','Mask plus eye/face protection for suction splash'],resources:['ALS intercept while BLS care starts','Additional trained help for two-person ventilation and movement'],equipment:['BVM, oxygen, suction and appropriate airway adjuncts','Naloxone per local scope/protocol'],extra:['Oral glucose for an unresponsive patient','Force the patient to walk to improve breathing'],contact:'A 27-year-old has no response to voice or an appropriate painful stimulus. A definite carotid pulse is present at 64/min. Breathing is 4/min and shallow, with gurgling secretions; no major bleeding.',avpu:'U',avpuWhy:'No response to either verbal or painful stimulation is U. Pinpoint pupils do not determine AVPU.',findings:['No life-threatening external bleeding found','Gurgling secretions in an unresponsive patient','RR 4 with shallow chest movement','Definite pulse 64/min; skin pale'],x:'Perform a rapid bleeding scan, then address the airway and inadequate breathing without delay.',a:'Open and clear the airway, suction as needed, and use an appropriate adjunct within scope.',b:'Provide effective assisted ventilation with a BVM and oxygen; do not wait for naloxone to work.',c:'Monitor pulse and perfusion, obtain vital signs and glucose when feasible, and reassess frequently.',why:['There is no catastrophic bleeding to control; X is a rapid check, not a reason to delay ventilation.','Gurgling indicates fluid threatening airway patency; a mask alone does not clear secretions.','A pulse with inadequate breathing calls for ventilation. Naloxone is an adjunct and must not replace respiratory support.','A palpable pulse is not a reason to stop monitoring: hypoxia can progress to arrest.'],transport:'Start transport after immediate airway and ventilation support; request ALS and continue monitoring even if naloxone improves responsiveness.',trend:'After ventilation and protocol-directed naloxone, the patient wakes and demands to stay home. He is drowsy again several minutes later.',next:'Reassess and support airway/breathing, reassess decision-making capacity, and involve medical control under refusal protocol; do not assume awakening makes refusal safe.',reason:'Opioid effects can recur. Clinical improvement alone does not establish capacity or eliminate ongoing respiratory risk.'},
 {id:'fall',dispatch:'Residence, 08:45. “Older adult fell and wants help up.” Family reports no bleeding.',scene:'The floor is dry and clear. There are no weapons or electrical hazards. A 79-year-old lies beside a chair; family says he takes an anticoagulant.',safe:'Confirm scene safety and patient access, use standard precautions, and assess before helping him stand.',unsafe:['Lift him immediately because this was dispatched as a lift assist.','Rule out major injury because there is no external bleeding.','Have him walk so you can decide whether transport is needed.'],clear:'You have a safe working area. Family confirms the fall happened about 20 minutes ago.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye protection available for anticipated exposure'],resources:['ALS for deteriorating perfusion without delaying transport','Additional crew for safe movement'],equipment:['Assessment kit with BP cuff and pulse oximeter','Stretcher and thermal protection'],extra:['Tourniquet applied to an uninjured limb','Standing orthostatic test before the primary assessment'],contact:'He has eyes open, spontaneously speaks, and is confused about the date. He reports abdominal and pelvic pain. RR 24, pulse 118, BP 96/60, pale cool skin. No catastrophic external bleeding is found.',avpu:'A',avpuWhy:'He is spontaneously awake and interactive: A on AVPU. Alert on AVPU does not mean oriented, neurologically normal, or stable.',findings:['No catastrophic external hemorrhage visible','Spontaneous speech, no airway obstruction','RR 24, equal rise, no obvious ventilatory failure','Pulse 118, BP 96/60, pale cool skin after a fall'],x:'Rapidly check for external bleeding, then recognize that absence of visible blood does not exclude internal hemorrhage.',a:'Monitor the currently patent airway and prepare to manage it if responsiveness worsens.',b:'Assess depth, effort and oxygenation; provide support according to findings and protocol.',c:'Suspect impaired perfusion/internal bleeding, minimize movement, prevent heat loss, and expedite appropriate trauma care.',why:['An external-bleeding scan cannot rule out hemorrhage in the chest, abdomen, pelvis, or retroperitoneum.','Spontaneous speech supports patency now, not a guarantee that it will remain adequate.','Rate alone does not determine the need for BVM ventilation.','Age, mechanism, anticoagulants, tachycardia, pain and poor perfusion make a benign lift-assist assumption unsafe.'],transport:'Prompt transport to an appropriate trauma-capable destination under regional guidance; do not delay for a complete on-scene history.',trend:'During packaging he becomes silent and opens his eyes only when you speak. BP is now 82/50.',next:'Update AVPU to V, repeat XABC immediately, communicate deterioration, and continue urgent transport with ALS coordination.',reason:'The trend is worsening perfusion and responsiveness, even without visible bleeding.'},
 {id:'stroke',dispatch:'Office, 10:30. “Confused employee.” Caller thinks he may have taken the wrong medication.',scene:'The office is safe. One 61-year-old is seated at a desk. A coworker says his speech changed suddenly; no trauma is reported.',safe:'Confirm safety, use standard precautions, and approach without assuming intoxication from the dispatch description.',unsafe:['Accept the medication explanation and defer a neurologic assessment.','Let coworkers drive him home before assessment.','Treat slurred speech as proof of opioid overdose.'],clear:'You establish patient contact in a clear area with a coworker available for history.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye protection available for anticipated exposure'],resources:['ALS coordination without delaying time-sensitive transport','Receiving stroke-center notification through local procedure'],equipment:['Glucose meter and neurologic assessment tools','Suction, airway kit and stretcher'],extra:['Give food or drink to test swallowing','Delay departure until every medication bottle is collected'],contact:'He is spontaneously awake, attempts to answer questions, has slurred speech and right arm drift, and follows simple commands. RR 18 with adequate rise, pulse 88, BP 176/94, SpO₂ 96%, glucose 108 mg/dL. No severe bleeding.',avpu:'A',avpuWhy:'Spontaneous wakefulness is A despite abnormal speech. AVPU is separate from a stroke screen or orientation assessment.',findings:['No severe external bleeding','Speech present; swallowing safety is not established','RR 18, adequate rise, SpO₂ 96%','Pulse 88, BP 176/94; focal neurologic deficit'],x:'Quickly exclude life-threatening bleeding, then proceed with the time-sensitive primary assessment.',a:'Monitor airway protection and secretion handling; do not give food, drink, or oral medication without appropriate assessment/protocol.',b:'Monitor breathing and oxygenation; do not give maximal oxygen automatically solely for suspected stroke with adequate oxygenation.',c:'Assess perfusion, document last-known-well and glucose, perform a validated stroke screen, and notify the receiving system.',why:['There is no external hemorrhage to treat in this presentation.','Speech does not prove safe swallowing; dysphagia can cause aspiration.','Oxygen is based on clinical need and local targets rather than diagnosis alone.','Last-known-well, glucose and focal findings guide time-sensitive stroke-system care.'],transport:'Promptly transport to a stroke-capable destination selected by local stroke-routing criteria; do not wait for symptoms to resolve.',trend:'A coworker says, “I found him at 10:20, but he was speaking normally at 09:05.”',next:'Record 09:05 as the last-known-well time, distinguish it from discovery time, and relay both with the neurologic findings.',reason:'Discovery time and last-known-well are different. Do not substitute the later discovery time.'},
 {id:'infection',dispatch:'Home, 18:40. “Fever and trouble breathing.” A household member has a confirmed transmissible respiratory infection.',scene:'The home is safe, but the patient is coughing heavily in a small room. Assisted ventilation or suction may become necessary.',safe:'Perform an exposure risk assessment, use indicated respiratory precautions, and improve ventilation when feasible without delaying needed care.',unsafe:['Enter without respiratory protection because only one person is sick.','Use a surgical mask as a guaranteed substitute for a fit-tested respirator during aerosol-risk procedures.','Refuse care until a laboratory diagnosis is available.'],clear:'You use agency-indicated respiratory precautions and arrange a clear patient-care area.',ppe:['Gloves and gown for anticipated secretion exposure','Fit-tested N95 or higher respirator plus eye protection for the anticipated aerosol-risk care'],resources:['ALS for respiratory compromise','Receiving-facility prealert including infection precautions'],equipment:['BVM, oxygen, suction and airway kit','Pulse oximeter, BP cuff and stretcher'],extra:['Patient mask that prevents assisted ventilation','Delay all respiratory support until a second diagnosis is obtained'],contact:'A 67-year-old does not respond to voice; she opens her eyes and withdraws only with an appropriate painful stimulus. RR 34 but shallow with poor chest rise; SpO₂ 84%, pulse 124, BP 88/54. No major external bleeding. Secretions collect in the mouth.',avpu:'P',avpuWhy:'She responds only to an appropriate painful stimulus: P. A high respiratory rate does not prove effective ventilation.',findings:['No life-threatening external bleeding','Secretions threaten airway patency','RR 34, shallow, poor chest rise, SpO₂ 84%','Pulse 124, BP 88/54, altered responsiveness'],x:'Quickly exclude severe external hemorrhage and move to the airway and ventilation threats.',a:'Open and clear the airway, suction as needed, and monitor protection of the airway.',b:'Assist inadequate ventilation with appropriate BVM technique and oxygen while maintaining indicated PPE.',c:'Treat poor perfusion, prevent temperature loss, obtain serial vitals, and expedite care with ALS coordination.',why:['No catastrophic external bleeding is present, so it must not distract from failing breathing.','A gurgling or contaminated airway requires clearing, not merely increasing oxygen flow.','The shallow, ineffective effort requires ventilation support despite tachypnea.','Hypotension and altered responsiveness indicate serious compromise; an exact infection diagnosis is not required before escalation.'],transport:'Prompt transport to an appropriate emergency department with ongoing respiratory support and infection prealert; avoid avoidable scene delays.',trend:'SpO₂ rises after ventilation, but BP falls to 78/46 and she no longer responds to voice or an appropriate painful stimulus.',next:'Update AVPU to U and reassess the entire primary assessment; do not treat the better oxygen number as overall recovery.',reason:'A single improving metric cannot outweigh worsening perfusion and mental status.'},
 {id:'co',dispatch:'Garage, 07:20. “Two people dizzy.” A gasoline generator has been running near an open garage door.',scene:'Two people are seated deep inside the garage. Your partner suggests that the open door makes entry safe. You do not have atmosphere-monitoring equipment or respiratory protection for a hazardous atmosphere.',safe:'Remain outside the hazardous area, request fire/HazMat assessment and safe removal; do not enter based on an open door.',unsafe:['Enter holding your breath for a quick rescue.','Wear an N95 and enter because it filters carbon monoxide.','Trust the open door and a normal finger pulse-ox reading as proof of safety.'],clear:'Fire personnel assess the atmosphere and safely remove both people to a safe area. You do not enter the garage. A second EMS unit takes the other patient.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye protection available for anticipated exposure'],resources:['Fire/HazMat for atmosphere assessment and safe removal','Additional ambulance for the second patient plus ALS coordination'],equipment:['Oxygen delivery and BVM equipment','Stretcher and assessment kit'],extra:['N95 as protection against carbon monoxide gas','Send an unprotected EMT back inside to retrieve belongings'],contact:'Your patient is 44, spontaneously awake but confused with headache and nausea. RR 22, pulse 112, BP 104/68. Conventional SpO₂ reads 99%. He speaks clearly and has adequate chest rise. No major bleeding.',avpu:'A',avpuWhy:'He is spontaneously awake: A, even though confusion is abnormal. AVPU is not an orientation score.',findings:['No severe external bleeding','Clear speech and patent airway','Adequate chest rise; conventional SpO₂ 99% despite suspected CO','Pulse 112, BP 104/68, confusion after shared exposure'],x:'Perform a rapid external-bleeding check without losing focus on the exposure emergency.',a:'Monitor airway patency and protection, with suction and ventilation equipment ready.',b:'Administer high-concentration oxygen for suspected CO exposure per protocol; do not use conventional SpO₂ to exclude poisoning.',c:'Assess perfusion and neurologic status, coordinate ALS/medical control, and monitor for deterioration.',why:['No hemorrhage is identified. The shared environmental exposure remains the key concern.','Current speech supports patency, but mental status may worsen.','Conventional pulse oximetry cannot reliably distinguish carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin.','Severity depends on the whole clinical picture, not a reassuring conventional oxygen-saturation reading.'],transport:'Transport for emergency evaluation with ongoing oxygen; consult medical control about destination and specialized care based on symptoms and local resources.',trend:'The patient says his headache is improving and asks to go inside to turn off the generator.',next:'Keep everyone out until fire personnel declare the area safe; continue assessment and recommended transport despite symptom improvement.',reason:'Improvement outside does not establish scene safety or rule out serious carbon monoxide exposure.'},
 {id:'asthma',dispatch:'School gym, 15:15. “Asthma attack.” An 11-year-old has been using an inhaler without relief.',scene:'The scene is safe and a teacher is present. The child is sitting upright. No injury, bleeding, or choking event is reported.',safe:'Confirm safety, use exposure-appropriate precautions, and assess the child without forcing a position change.',unsafe:['Lay the child flat immediately regardless of breathing comfort.','Assume the inhaler resolved the emergency before seeing the patient.','Require the child to walk to the ambulance to assess exercise tolerance.'],clear:'The teacher creates space. You can assess the child in the position she tolerates.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye protection available for anticipated secretion exposure'],resources:['ALS for severe respiratory distress','Additional crew for supported movement and equipment'],equipment:['Pediatric-appropriate BVM, oxygen, suction and airway kit','Pulse oximeter and permitted bronchodilator equipment'],extra:['Paper bag for rebreathing','Oral fluid challenge during severe respiratory distress'],contact:'She is spontaneously awake and can speak only one or two words. RR 36, marked retractions, poor air movement and fatigue; pulse 138, BP 110/72, SpO₂ 88%. She follows commands. No severe bleeding.',avpu:'A',avpuWhy:'She is spontaneously awake and follows commands: A. Severe breathing compromise can occur while a patient is still A on AVPU.',findings:['No life-threatening external bleeding','Airway currently open; speech limited by respiratory distress','RR 36, retractions, poor air movement, fatigue, SpO₂ 88%','Pulse 138, BP 110/72; ongoing risk of hypoxic deterioration'],x:'Rapidly exclude major external bleeding and prioritize the respiratory emergency.',a:'Maintain the airway and tolerated position; prepare for loss of airway protection if fatigue worsens.',b:'Support oxygenation, assess ventilation continuously, give permitted bronchodilator care, and assist ventilation if effort becomes inadequate.',c:'Monitor perfusion and repeated vitals while arranging prompt transport and ALS support.',why:['There is no severe hemorrhage to control.','Do not force a flat position in a conscious child who is struggling to breathe; reassess as status changes.','Poor air movement and fatigue can signal severe obstruction. Reduced wheezing does not necessarily mean improvement.','An initially maintained blood pressure does not make severe respiratory distress low acuity.'],transport:'Begin prompt transport with ongoing respiratory treatment and ALS coordination; do not stay to exhaust repeated on-scene treatments.',trend:'RR falls to 12 with very shallow breaths, chest rise decreases, and she opens her eyes only when spoken to.',next:'Recognize fatigue and impending respiratory failure, update AVPU to V, and provide effective assisted ventilation with appropriately sized equipment.',reason:'A slower respiratory rate with reduced effort and responsiveness is deterioration, not recovery.'},
 {id:'arrest',dispatch:'Community center, 19:00. “Fainted, making snoring sounds.” Staff are bringing an AED.',scene:'The floor is dry, no trauma or other hazard is apparent, and staff have cleared room around the patient.',safe:'Confirm scene safety, use standard precautions, and rapidly assess responsiveness, breathing and pulse.',unsafe:['Assume snoring or gasping means adequate breathing and begin a full SAMPLE history.','Wait for ALS before checking the patient.','Carry the patient outside before assessing for cardiac arrest.'],clear:'You and your partner reach the patient safely; an AED is beside you.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye/face protection for anticipated resuscitation exposure'],resources:['ALS and additional resuscitation personnel','A trained helper to operate the AED and rotate compressions'],equipment:['AED and BVM with oxygen','Suction and airway adjunct kit'],extra:['Routine stair-chair movement before initial resuscitation','Oral glucose while unresponsive'],contact:'A 58-year-old does not respond to voice or an appropriate painful stimulus. There are occasional gasps and no definite pulse during a simultaneous breathing/pulse check completed within 10 seconds. No catastrophic bleeding.',avpu:'U',avpuWhy:'No response to voice or pain is U. Occasional gasps are not normal breathing.',findings:['No catastrophic external hemorrhage','Unresponsive and unable to protect the airway','Occasional gasps, not normal breathing','No definite pulse within a check of no more than 10 seconds'],x:'Rapidly exclude catastrophic external bleeding; recognize cardiac arrest and start CPR/AED care rather than a prolonged XABC sequence.',a:'Manage the airway as part of resuscitation with appropriate adjuncts and minimal interruption of compressions.',b:'Provide ventilations using current BLS guidance with effective chest rise and minimal compression interruptions.',c:'Start high-quality CPR and use the AED promptly; do not delay for routine transport or a full blood-pressure measurement.',why:['XABC is a prioritization aid, not a replacement for the cardiac-arrest algorithm.','Airway interventions must not create avoidable interruptions in high-quality CPR.','Gasping does not substitute for ventilation; coordinate compressions and breaths under current guidance.','No definite pulse plus unresponsiveness and abnormal breathing requires CPR and early defibrillation.'],transport:'Prioritize immediate on-scene CPR/AED and ALS resuscitation; transport timing follows resuscitation protocol and circumstances, not automatic immediate loading.',trend:'The AED says “no shock advised.” The patient remains unresponsive, has no normal breathing and no definite pulse.',next:'Resume CPR promptly and follow AED prompts; “no shock advised” does not mean circulation has returned.',reason:'Nonshockable arrest still requires resuscitation. The AED message is not proof of a pulse.'}
];
SCENARIO_CASES.push({id:'ankle',dispatch:'Park trailhead, 11:50. “Runner fell, severe ankle pain.” Caller requests the fastest ambulance response.',scene:'A 29-year-old is seated on a bench at the accessible trailhead after twisting an ankle. No head impact, hazardous terrain, traffic exposure or other injuries are reported.',safe:'Confirm safe access and standard precautions, then assess the patient and mechanism rather than assuming dispatch urgency equals clinical instability.',unsafe:['Request helicopter evacuation before assessing trailhead access or patient condition.','Make the patient walk to prove the injury is minor.','Skip the primary assessment because the caller described an isolated ankle injury.'],clear:'You confirm safe access and a low-energy twisting mechanism. The patient can be assessed without standing.',ppe:['Gloves','Eye protection available for anticipated exposure'],resources:['Partner assistance for safe non-weight-bearing movement','Additional lifting help only if assessment shows it is needed'],equipment:['Splinting supplies and assessment kit','Stretcher or suitable seated movement device after assessment'],extra:['Automatic ALS or helicopter activation solely for a pain score of 9/10','Require weight-bearing before arranging movement'],contact:'He spontaneously describes the fall clearly. RR 20, normal depth, pulse 102, BP 128/78, SpO₂ 98%, warm dry skin. No major bleeding, airway problem or other primary-assessment threat. The ankle is swollen; distal pulse, sensation and movement are initially intact.',avpu:'A',avpuWhy:'Spontaneous, interactive behavior is A. Pain severity does not determine AVPU or transport urgency by itself.',findings:['No severe external bleeding','Clear full-sentence speech without airway sounds','RR 20 with normal depth and SpO₂ 98%','Pulse 102, BP 128/78, warm dry skin and intact distal perfusion'],x:'Rapidly exclude life-threatening bleeding, then proceed with the primary and focused assessment.',a:'Continue to monitor the currently patent airway; no airway adjunct is indicated from these findings.',b:'Assess and monitor breathing; routine oxygen or BVM assistance is not indicated by the current findings.',c:'Perform a focused injury examination, check distal pulse/sensation/movement before and after splinting, and reassess comfort and perfusion.',why:['The primary assessment is still needed, but there is no catastrophic bleeding to treat.','A patent airway in a responsive patient does not need a prophylactic adjunct.','Normal depth, effort and oxygenation do not justify routine assisted ventilation or oxygen solely because the patient is injured.','Pain and mild tachycardia alone do not establish shock. Distal neurovascular checks and safe immobilization are priorities.'],transport:'Complete the focused assessment and arrange appropriate non-emergent transport under local policy while monitoring; escalate if new threats appear.',trend:'After splinting, he reports new numbness. The toes look paler and the distal pulse is harder to feel.',next:'Immediately reassess distal neurovascular status and splint fit, correct constriction within training/protocol, and escalate transport/consultation for persistent compromise.',reason:'A previously stable injury can develop a time-sensitive limb-perfusion problem. Compare before-and-after findings rather than relying on the initial low-acuity impression.'});
const SCENARIO_STAGE_NAMES=['Scene safety','PPE · resources · equipment','AVPU','Map XABC findings','X · hemorrhage','A · airway','B · breathing','C · circulation','Transport decision','Reassessment'];
let dispatchRound=null,dispatchBag=[],dispatchPrevious=null;
function dispatchChoice(prompt,correct,wrong,explanation){return {kind:'choice',prompt,correct,options:shuffle([correct,...wrong]),explanation}}
function dispatchBuild(c){const kits=[...c.ppe.map(text=>({text,zone:'ppe'})),...c.resources.map(text=>({text,zone:'resources'})),...c.equipment.map(text=>({text,zone:'equipment'})),...c.extra.map(text=>({text,zone:'defer'}))].map((x,id)=>({...x,id:'kit-'+id}));const findings=c.findings.map((text,i)=>({text,zone:['X','A','B','C'][i],id:'finding-'+i}));return [
 dispatchChoice('What must happen before patient contact?',c.safe,c.unsafe,'Dispatch information is provisional. '+c.clear),
 {kind:'sort',prompt:'Assign every card: required PPE, requested resources, equipment to bring, or an inappropriate/delaying action. Select all items by placing every card.',cards:shuffle(kits),zones:{ppe:'Wear / prepare PPE',resources:'Request / coordinate resources',equipment:'Bring equipment',defer:'Inappropriate or avoidable delay'},explanation:'Base PPE on anticipated exposure and choose help/equipment for the actual scene. Resources support immediate EMT care; they do not replace it. Items in the fourth group are inappropriate actions as written, not a general ban on the equipment.'},
 {kind:'text',prompt:'Enter the patient’s initial AVPU category (A, V, P, or U). Use the observed response, not the dispatch complaint.',correct:c.avpu,aliases:{A:['a','alert'],V:['v','verbal','voice','responds to voice','responds to verbal stimuli'],P:['p','pain','responds to pain','responds to painful stimuli'],U:['u','unresponsive']}[c.avpu],explanation:c.avpuWhy},
 {kind:'sort',prompt:'Place each finding under the primary-assessment component it belongs to. This sorting task identifies threats; it does not mean treatments must wait for a rigid sequence.',cards:shuffle(findings),zones:{X:'X · catastrophic external bleeding',A:'A · airway patency/protection',B:'B · breathing/ventilation',C:'C · circulation/perfusion'},explanation:'Look for life threats and treat them when found. Teams can work in parallel. For an unresponsive patient with abnormal breathing, assess breathing and pulse together and use the cardiac-arrest pathway when indicated.'},
 dispatchChoice('What is the best X action for these findings?',c.x,['Complete a detailed head-to-toe examination before deciding whether severe bleeding exists.','Treat the absence of visible blood as proof that hemorrhage is excluded.','Apply a tourniquet prophylactically before identifying an uncontrolled extremity hemorrhage.'],c.why[0]),
 dispatchChoice('What is the most appropriate airway decision now?',c.a,['Insert an OPA solely because the patient is confused, without assessing gag reflex or tolerance.','Consider the airway permanently secure once the patient has spoken one word.','Finish a detailed history before acting on abnormal airway sounds or loss of airway protection.'],c.why[1]),
 dispatchChoice('Which breathing plan best fits the current findings?',c.b,c.id==='arrest'?['Treat occasional gasps as normal breathing and withhold ventilations.','Pause CPR for a prolonged respiratory examination before providing breaths.','Hyperventilate rapidly to compensate for poor circulation.']:c.id==='co'?['Use the 99% conventional SpO₂ to exclude hypoxia from carbon monoxide and withhold oxygen.','Provide assisted ventilation solely because exposure occurred, despite currently adequate effort.','Delay oxygen until a definitive carboxyhemoglobin result is available.']:['Choose oxygen alone even if chest rise and respiratory effort are ineffective.','Use respiratory rate alone to decide that ventilation is adequate.','Provide maximal oxygen and rapid BVM breaths automatically, regardless of effort and oxygenation.'],c.why[2]),
 dispatchChoice('What is the best circulation/perfusion plan?',c.c,c.id==='arrest'?['Load the patient before starting compressions because all unstable patients need immediate departure.','Obtain a complete BP and medication history before starting CPR.','Wait for ALS to confirm cardiac arrest before applying the AED.']:['Delay movement until a complete secondary assessment and all paperwork are finished.','Use one blood-pressure reading alone to determine that perfusion is adequate.','Wait for the exact diagnosis before treating impaired perfusion or reassessing vital signs.'],c.why[3]),
 dispatchChoice('Choose the transport and resource decision. Assume standard local destination options are available; do not infer lights-and-sirens use.',c.transport,c.id==='arrest'?['Immediately move to the ambulance before starting CPR or applying the AED.','Wait without BLS care for ALS to decide whether resuscitation is indicated.','Cancel all transport planning if the AED does not advise a shock.']:['Wait on scene for ALS before continuing any BLS treatment or starting transport.','Use the closest building labeled “clinic” regardless of patient condition or facility capability.','Finish a complete history and secondary examination before making any transport decision.'],'Treat immediate threats, select an appropriate receiving capability under local routing, and avoid preventable delays. Priority is separate from the decision to use warning devices. '+(c.id==='arrest'?'Cardiac arrest requires immediate CPR/AED; routine loading first is harmful.':c.id==='ankle'?'Current findings support focused care and non-emergent transport rather than automatic high-acuity escalation.':'')),
 dispatchChoice('New information: '+c.trend+' What do you do next?',c.next,['Continue the original plan without repeating the primary assessment because treatment has already started.','Downgrade urgency based on the single most reassuring sign and stop repeated assessments.','Delay all treatment decisions until a complete set of new vital signs is obtained.'],c.reason)
 ]}
function dispatchNormalize(v){return String(v??'').toLowerCase().trim().replace(/[.]/g,'').replace(/\s+/g,' ')}
function dispatchScore(stage,answer){if(stage.kind==='choice')return {earned:answer===stage.correct?1:0,total:1};if(stage.kind==='text')return {earned:stage.aliases.some(a=>dispatchNormalize(a)===dispatchNormalize(answer))?1:0,total:1};return {earned:stage.cards.filter(c=>answer?.[c.id]===c.zone).length,total:stage.cards.length}}
function dispatchStart(){if(!dispatchBag.length){dispatchBag=shuffle(SCENARIO_CASES.map(c=>c.id));if(dispatchBag.length>1&&dispatchBag.at(-1)===dispatchPrevious)[dispatchBag[0],dispatchBag[dispatchBag.length-1]]=[dispatchBag.at(-1),dispatchBag[0]]}const id=dispatchBag.pop(),c=SCENARIO_CASES.find(x=>x.id===id);dispatchPrevious=id;dispatchRound={case:c,stages:dispatchTune(c,dispatchBuild(c)),step:0,answers:{},results:{},selected:null,done:false,saved:false,message:''};dispatchPractice()}
function dispatchHistory(){try{const x=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('emt-dispatch-history-v1')||'[]');return Array.isArray(x)?x.filter(a=>a&&Number.isFinite(a.score)&&typeof a.date==='string').slice(-30):[]}catch{return []}}
function dispatchPractice(){if(!dispatchRound){const history=dispatchHistory();app.innerHTML=heading('Dispatch to decision.','Unfolding EMT calls with plausible distractors, hidden findings, and changing priorities.')+`<section class="hero"><div><span class="tag">9 ORIGINAL CALLS · MIXED INTERACTIONS</span><h2>You’re dispatched. What comes next?</h2><p>Scene safety, loadout, AVPU, XABC, transport, and reassessment. Sort cards, type your assessment, and commit to decisions before seeing feedback.</p><button class="primary" onclick="dispatchStart()">Take a random dispatch →</button></div></section><section class="contentcard"><h2>How the challenge works</h2><p>Each call has 10 stages. Your first committed answer counts; feedback reveals the reasoning. Place every card, including distractors. Work from the findings rather than guessing a diagnosis. Calls do not repeat until you have drawn all nine in this visit.</p><p>AVPU and orientation are different. XABC helps organize threats; cardiac arrest requires the BLS resuscitation pathway, and a team may treat threats simultaneously. Transport priority does not automatically mean lights and sirens.</p><p>Original study scenarios informed by national education and assessment guidance. Equipment, interventions, infection precautions and destinations follow local scope/protocol.</p></section><section class="contentcard"><h2>Your recent completed calls</h2>${history.length?history.slice(-5).reverse().map(x=>`<p><b>${x.score}%</b> · ${esc(x.dispatch)} · ${esc(new Date(x.date).toLocaleDateString())}</p>`).join(''):'<p>No completed calls saved in this browser yet.</p>'}</section>`+dispatchReferences();return}if(dispatchRound.done){dispatchSummary();return}const r=dispatchRound,c=r.case,i=r.step,q=r.stages[i],graded=r.results[i],answer=r.answers[i];app.innerHTML=heading('Dispatch to decision.',`Stage ${i+1} of 10 · ${SCENARIO_STAGE_NAMES[i]}`)+`<div class="dispatch-progress" aria-label="Call progress">${SCENARIO_STAGE_NAMES.map((name,j)=>`<span class="${j===i?'current':r.results[j]?'complete':''}">${j+1}. ${esc(name)}</span>`).join('')}</div><section class="dispatch-brief"><div class="eyebrow">DISPATCH · PROVISIONAL INFORMATION</div><h2>${esc(c.dispatch)}</h2><p><b>On arrival:</b> ${esc(c.scene)}</p>${i>0?`<p><b>Access update:</b> ${esc(c.clear)}</p>`:''}${i>=2?`<p class="dispatch-findings"><b>Patient contact:</b> ${esc(c.contact)}</p>`:''}</section><section class="contentcard"><span class="tag">${q.kind==='sort'?'DRAG, TAP OR KEYBOARD':q.kind==='text'?'FILL IN THE BLANK':'SINGLE BEST DECISION'}</span><h2>${esc(q.prompt)}</h2>${dispatchInteraction(q,answer,!!graded)}<p id="dispatch-message" role="status" aria-live="polite">${esc(r.message)}</p>${graded?dispatchFeedback(q,answer,graded):'<button class="primary" onclick="dispatchCommit()">Commit answer & get feedback</button>'}${graded?`<button class="primary" onclick="dispatchNext()">${i===9?'Finish call & debrief':'Continue to next stage →'}</button>`:''}</section><div class="toolbar"><button onclick="dispatchStart()">End this call & draw another</button><button onclick="dispatchLobby()">Scenario overview</button></div>`+dispatchReferences()}
function dispatchInteraction(q,answer,locked){if(q.kind==='choice')return `<fieldset class="dispatch-options"><legend class="sr-only">Choose one decision</legend>${q.options.map((text,k)=>`<label class="dispatch-option"><input type="radio" name="dispatch-choice" value="${k}" ${answer===text?'checked':''} ${locked?'disabled':''} onchange="dispatchChoose(${k})"><span>${esc(text)}</span></label>`).join('')}</fieldset>`;if(q.kind==='text')return `<label for="dispatch-text">AVPU category</label><input id="dispatch-text" class="dispatch-text" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="characters" spellcheck="false" value="${esc(answer||'')}" ${locked?'disabled':''} oninput="dispatchType(this.value)" placeholder="Enter category"><p class="muted">Commit before seeing the classification and explanation.</p>`;const placements=answer||{};const cardHtml=card=>`<button type="button" class="dispatch-card ${dispatchRound.selected===card.id?'picked':''}" ${locked?'disabled':''} draggable="${!locked}" aria-pressed="${dispatchRound.selected===card.id}" onclick="dispatchPick('${card.id}')" ondragstart="dispatchDrag(event,'${card.id}')">${esc(card.text)}${placements[card.id]?`<small>${locked?'':'Select to move or return to bank'}</small>`:''}</button>`;return `<p>Drag each card, or select it and choose a destination. Cards can be moved before you commit. Every card needs a destination.</p><div class="dispatch-bank" ondragover="event.preventDefault()" ondrop="dispatchDrop(event,'bank')"><h3>Unassigned cards (${q.cards.filter(x=>!placements[x.id]).length})</h3><button ${locked?'disabled':''} onclick="dispatchPlace('bank')">Return selected card here</button><div class="dispatch-cards">${q.cards.filter(x=>!placements[x.id]).map(cardHtml).join('')}</div></div><div class="dispatch-zones">${Object.entries(q.zones).map(([zone,label])=>`<div class="dispatch-zone" ondragover="event.preventDefault()" ondrop="dispatchDrop(event,'${zone}')"><button class="dispatch-zone-target" ${locked?'disabled':''} onclick="dispatchPlace('${zone}')">${esc(label)}<small>Place selected card here</small></button>${q.cards.filter(x=>placements[x.id]===zone).map(cardHtml).join('')}</div>`).join('')}</div>`}
function dispatchCurrent(){return dispatchRound&&!dispatchRound.done?dispatchRound.stages[dispatchRound.step]:null}
function dispatchChoose(k){const q=dispatchCurrent();if(q?.kind!=='choice'||dispatchRound.results[dispatchRound.step]||!Number.isInteger(k)||!q.options[k])return;dispatchRound.answers[dispatchRound.step]=q.options[k]}
function dispatchType(v){if(dispatchCurrent()?.kind==='text'&&!dispatchRound.results[dispatchRound.step])dispatchRound.answers[dispatchRound.step]=v}
function dispatchPick(id){const q=dispatchCurrent();if(q?.kind!=='sort'||dispatchRound.results[dispatchRound.step]||!q.cards.some(c=>c.id===id))return;dispatchRound.selected=dispatchRound.selected===id?null:id;dispatchRound.message=dispatchRound.selected?'Card selected. Choose a destination heading.':'Selection cleared.';dispatchPractice()}
function dispatchPlace(zone,id=dispatchRound?.selected){const q=dispatchCurrent();if(q?.kind!=='sort'||dispatchRound.results[dispatchRound.step]||!q.cards.some(c=>c.id===id)||(zone!=='bank'&&!Object.hasOwn(q.zones,zone)))return;const answer=dispatchRound.answers[dispatchRound.step]??={};if(zone==='bank')delete answer[id];else answer[id]=zone;dispatchRound.selected=null;dispatchRound.message='Card moved. Continue sorting or commit when ready.';dispatchPractice()}
function dispatchDrag(event,id){const q=dispatchCurrent();if(q?.kind!=='sort'||dispatchRound.results[dispatchRound.step]||!q.cards.some(c=>c.id===id)){event.preventDefault();return}event.dataTransfer.setData('application/x-emt-dispatch',id);event.dataTransfer.effectAllowed='move'}
function dispatchDrop(event,zone){event.preventDefault();dispatchPlace(zone,event.dataTransfer.getData('application/x-emt-dispatch'))}
function dispatchFeedback(q,answer,result){return `<div class="dispatch-feedback" role="status"><h3>${result.earned===result.total?'Strong decision':'Review the reasoning'} · ${result.earned}/${result.total}</h3>${q.kind==='sort'?`<ul>${q.cards.map(card=>`<li>${answer?.[card.id]===card.zone?'✓':'↻'} ${esc(card.text)} → <b>${esc(q.zones[card.zone])}</b></li>`).join('')}</ul>`:`<p><b>Best answer:</b> ${esc(q.correct)}</p>`}<p>${esc(q.explanation)}</p><p class="muted">First attempt recorded. The next stage assumes the recommended care/access state so you can keep practicing.</p></div>`}
function dispatchCommit(){const r=dispatchRound,q=dispatchCurrent();if(!q||r.results[r.step])return;const a=r.answers[r.step];if(q.kind==='sort'&&q.cards.some(c=>!Object.hasOwn(q.zones,a?.[c.id]))||q.kind==='choice'&&!q.options.includes(a)||q.kind==='text'&&!String(a||'').trim()){r.message='Complete this response first. Place every card, choose one decision, or enter your category.';dispatchPractice();return}r.results[r.step]=dispatchScore(q,a);r.message='First attempt locked. Review feedback before continuing.';r.selected=null;dispatchPractice();document.getElementById('dispatch-message')?.scrollIntoView({block:'center',behavior:'smooth'})}
function dispatchNext(){if(!dispatchRound?.results[dispatchRound.step])return;dispatchRound.message='';dispatchRound.selected=null;if(dispatchRound.step===9){dispatchRound.done=true;dispatchSummary()}else{dispatchRound.step++;dispatchPractice()}document.querySelector('#app').scrollIntoView({block:'start',behavior:'smooth'})}
function dispatchTotals(r){const results=Object.values(r.results),earned=results.reduce((n,x)=>n+x.earned,0),total=results.reduce((n,x)=>n+x.total,0);return {earned,total,score:total?Math.round(earned/total*100):0}}
function dispatchSummary(){const r=dispatchRound;if(!r?.done)return;const t=dispatchTotals(r);let saved=true;if(!r.saved){try{const history=dispatchHistory();history.push({date:new Date().toISOString(),dispatch:r.case.dispatch,score:t.score});localStorage.setItem('emt-dispatch-history-v1',JSON.stringify(history.slice(-30)));r.saved=true}catch{saved=false}}app.innerHTML=heading('Call debrief.',r.case.dispatch)+`<section class="contentcard"><span class="tag">FIRST-ATTEMPT RESULTS</span><div class="result-score">${t.score}%</div><h2>${t.earned} of ${t.total} scored decisions/card placements</h2><p><b>Priority decisions to review:</b> ${[0,4,5,6,7,8,9].filter(i=>r.results[i].earned<r.results[i].total).map(i=>SCENARIO_STAGE_NAMES[i]).join(", ")||"None missed this round"}.</p><p>This is practice feedback, not a certification score or a clinical-readiness measure. Card-sorting stages award one point per correct card; each other stage is one point.</p><div class="toolbar"><button class="primary" onclick="dispatchStart()">Next random dispatch →</button><button onclick="dispatchReplay()">Retry this call</button><button onclick="dispatchLobby()">Scenario overview</button></div><p class="storage-note">${saved?'Completed result saved in this browser.':'This browser could not save the result.'}</p></section>${r.stages.map((q,i)=>`<details class="contentcard" ${r.results[i].earned<r.results[i].total?'open':''}><summary>${i+1}. ${SCENARIO_STAGE_NAMES[i]} · ${r.results[i].earned}/${r.results[i].total}</summary><h3>${esc(q.prompt)}</h3>${q.kind==='sort'?'':`<p>Your answer: ${esc(r.answers[i])}</p>`}${dispatchFeedback(q,r.answers[i],r.results[i])}</details>`).join('')}`+dispatchReferences()}
function dispatchReplay(){if(!dispatchRound)return;const c=dispatchRound.case;dispatchRound={case:c,stages:dispatchTune(c,dispatchBuild(c)),step:0,answers:{},results:{},selected:null,done:false,saved:false,message:''};dispatchPractice()}
function dispatchLobby(){dispatchRound=null;dispatchPractice()}
function dispatchReferences(){return `<p class="source">Educational scenarios · Follow current local protocols and instructor guidance. References: <a href="https://www.ems.gov/assets/EMS_Education_Standards_2021_Updated2_24_25forEO.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National EMS Education Standards</a> · <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/isolation-precautions/appendix-a-table-4.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC exposure-based PPE</a> · <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/carbon-monoxide/hcp/clinical-guidance/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC carbon monoxide guidance</a> · <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/opioid-overdose" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO opioid-overdose care</a> · <a href="https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/cpr-and-ecc-guidelines/adult-basic-life-support" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHA adult BLS</a> · <a href="https://www.facs.org/quality-programs/trauma/systems/field-triage-guidelines/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trauma-system destination guidance</a></p>`}
// More competitive alternatives target common priority errors rather than trivia.
function dispatchTune(c,stages){const alternatives={
 saw:{4:['Maintain pressure and apply a pressure dressing for several more minutes before considering a tourniquet.','Elevate the leg and complete distal motor/sensory checks before controlling the spurting hemorrhage.','Package for immediate departure and control the bleeding only after loading.'],6:['Apply high-flow oxygen and begin rapid BVM ventilation because RR is above the normal range.','Withhold all respiratory assessment because SpO₂ is 95%.','Treat the respiratory rate as the primary problem and delay hemorrhage control to coach slower breathing.'],8:['Transport to the nearest non-trauma emergency department regardless of regional trauma routing.','Hold on scene for the ALS unit before departing, even if hemorrhage is controlled and intercept is available.','Stay to splint the injury and obtain a complete SAMPLE history before making a destination decision.'],9:['Keep the same AVPU category because he responded to voice at first contact.','Remove or loosen the tourniquet to improve distal perfusion before reassessing breathing.','Continue oxygen by mask alone and focus on obtaining another blood pressure.']},
 overdose:{5:['Place an OPA immediately without assessing airway secretions, gag reflex, or tolerance.','Place oxygen over the mouth and nose and leave suction for after naloxone.','Treat the gurgling as normal snoring and move directly to a detailed pupil examination.'],6:['Administer naloxone first, then wait a few minutes before deciding whether BVM ventilation is necessary.','Use a nonrebreather alone because a pulse is present and some breaths are occurring.','Start chest compressions solely because the patient is unresponsive, despite a definite pulse.'],8:['Allow refusal once he opens his eyes after naloxone, without assessing capacity or recurrence risk.','Keep him on scene until completely awake before starting transport, regardless of respiratory status.','Delay basic airway care until ALS can select the receiving facility.'],9:['Accept the original refusal as final because he was awake when he first requested it.','Give oral glucose while he is drowsy without assessing his ability to swallow.','Assume recurrent drowsiness is normal sleep and stop frequent respiratory assessment.']},
 fall:{7:['Treat BP 96/60 as reassuring because it is above the usual adult hypotension threshold.','Attribute confusion to age and prioritize helping him back into the chair.','Focus on external bleeding alone because internal hemorrhage cannot be confirmed in the field.'],8:['Handle as a low-priority lift assist because no external hemorrhage or open fracture is visible.','Take him to a routine clinic because the fall was from standing height.','Delay departure to obtain standing vital signs and assess walking stability.'],9:['Keep AVPU at A because the patient is still capable of answering after repeated prompting.','Attribute the lower BP to the cuff and wait for a third reading before reassessing.','Raise him to standing to evaluate whether the low pressure is positional.']},
 stroke:{7:['Treat the elevated blood pressure as the first problem and attempt to normalize it before transport.','Classify this as medication intoxication because that was the caller’s explanation.','Use a normal glucose to exclude stroke and perform a full medication review on scene.'],8:['Transport without stroke prealert because he is awake and has a normal oxygen saturation.','Use symptom-discovery time as the onset time when selecting stroke-system routing.','Wait for a second stroke screen to normalize before deciding whether urgent transport is needed.'],9:['Use 10:20 as last-known-well because that is when the abnormal speech was first discovered.','Use 10:30 as last-known-well because that is the dispatch time.','Report only the estimated duration of transport; last-known-well can be collected at the hospital.']},
 infection:{6:['Treat RR 34 as adequate spontaneous ventilation and use a nonrebreather without assessing chest rise.','Try a less invasive oxygen device first and wait for the saturation to improve despite ineffective chest rise.','Defer assisted ventilation until an advanced airway can be placed by ALS.'],8:['Delay departure until the exact infectious agent is identified for hospital placement.','Continue repeated on-scene oxygen-device trials despite poor ventilation and falling responsiveness.','Avoid hospital prealert because infection status is not yet laboratory confirmed.'],9:['Classify the overall trend as improvement because the oxygen saturation increased.','Continue the same respiratory plan without checking pulse or perfusion because ventilation has been started.','Wait for another oxygen-saturation reading before checking whether the airway remains protected.']},
 co:{0:['Enter wearing a fit-tested N95 with a partner because the door is open and the patients are visible.','Ask the less symptomatic patient to turn off the generator and bring the other person outside.','Send one EMT in with a portable oxygen cylinder while the second EMT remains outside.'],8:['Keep him outside for a few minutes and cancel transport when his headache starts to improve.','Base destination and urgency solely on the conventional pulse-ox reading of 99%.','Wait without oxygen for a field laboratory confirmation before starting transport.'],9:['Allow a brief reentry if he feels well enough and someone watches from the door.','Send him inside wearing an N95 because the generator only needs to be switched off.','Use improved headache and normal conventional SpO₂ as confirmation that reentry is safe.']},
 asthma:{6:['Interpret reduced air movement as less bronchospasm and reassess after a brief rest without escalation.','Give another inhaler treatment and postpone preparations for assisted ventilation despite fatigue.','Use the elevated rate alone to conclude that minute ventilation must be sufficient.'],8:['Stay on scene until wheezing disappears, then decide whether transport is still needed.','Make the child walk to the ambulance because the initial blood pressure is maintained.','Use routine low-priority transport because AVPU is A at first contact.'],9:['Interpret RR 12 as normalization and reduce treatment intensity.','Continue coaching slow breathing because the oxygen saturation is the only determinant of ventilation.','Keep giving inhaled medication while postponing BVM support despite very shallow breaths.']},
 arrest:{5:['Complete suction and advanced airway preparation before beginning compressions or AED use.','Move the patient to the stretcher to obtain a better airway position before starting CPR.','Wait for the AED to analyze before opening the airway or beginning any resuscitation.'],8:['Perform a full set of vital signs before deciding whether to resuscitate in place or transport.','Carry to the ambulance immediately so CPR can begin on the way to hospital.','Complete only rescue breaths while awaiting ALS because the patient is still gasping.'],9:['Check BP for a full minute to determine whether the no-shock message indicates return of circulation.','Stop CPR and observe because the AED has ruled out the need for resuscitation.','Begin loading immediately and defer compressions until the next AED analysis.']}
 }[c.id]||{};for(const [i,wrong] of Object.entries(alternatives)){const q=stages[Number(i)];if(q.kind==='choice')q.options=shuffle([q.correct,...wrong])}return stages}
